WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.480
Before a cell divides, it has to copy

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:05.680
all of its DNA: billions of letters, in

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:08.480
your own cells, packed into a nucleus far

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:10.320
smaller than a pinhead.

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:12.240
Copy a book that long and the copy

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:13.520
would drift.

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:17.040
Yet your cells copy DNA almost perfectly.

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:19.680
How, and what happens to the original?

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:23.460
DNA is a long chain of small units

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:25.140
called nucleotides.

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:29.540
Each has three parts: a sugar called deoxyribose,

00:00:29.620 --> 00:00:32.340
a phosphate group, which holds phosphorus and oxygen

00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:35.460
atoms, and a nitrogenous base, the part that

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:36.780
carries the letter.

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:39.780
The sugars and phosphates link in a backbone,

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:41.700
and the bases hang off it, in four

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:45.060
kinds: A, T, C and G.

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:47.940
The order of those letters is the information

00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:49.300
a cell stores.

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:53.940
DNA has two strands twisted around each other,

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:56.300
a shape called a double helix.

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:58.820
The backbones run on the outside, and the

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:01.540
bases point inward and meet, but not at

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:02.660
random.

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:05.940
A always pairs with T, and C always

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:08.900
pairs with G, because only those pairs fit

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:11.380
together, like jigsaw pieces.

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:14.260
This is complementary base pairing.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:17.780
Weak attractions called hydrogen bonds hold each pair:

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:20.660
two between A and T, three between C

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:21.220
and G.

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:24.660
To remember the pairs: Apple in the Tree,

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:27.060
Car in the Garage, in DNA.

00:01:28.267 --> 00:01:30.746
Each sugar joins to the next phosphate on

00:01:30.827 --> 00:01:33.546
one particular side, so a strand has a

00:01:33.627 --> 00:01:35.786
start end and a finish end, like an

00:01:35.867 --> 00:01:36.826
arrow.

00:01:36.907 --> 00:01:39.706
The start is called the 5' end, said

00:01:39.867 --> 00:01:42.426
five prime, and the finish is the 3'

00:01:42.507 --> 00:01:44.506
end, said three prime.

00:01:45.227 --> 00:01:47.466
In a double helix the two strands point

00:01:47.547 --> 00:01:51.626
in opposite directions: one runs 5' to 3',

00:01:51.707 --> 00:01:54.026
its partner 3' to 5'.

00:01:54.507 --> 00:01:57.386
We call them antiparallel, like the two lanes

00:01:57.427 --> 00:01:58.106
of a road.

00:01:59.334 --> 00:02:02.373
Because A only pairs with T, and C

00:02:02.534 --> 00:02:05.653
only with G, one strand fixes the order

00:02:05.734 --> 00:02:06.693
of the other.

00:02:07.014 --> 00:02:10.213
If one strand reads A, T, C, G,

00:02:10.294 --> 00:02:13.013
its partner has to read T, A, G,

00:02:13.094 --> 00:02:13.493
C.

00:02:14.134 --> 00:02:16.933
So each strand holds the information to rebuild

00:02:17.014 --> 00:02:17.733
its partner.

00:02:18.374 --> 00:02:21.093
Used like this, it is called a template,

00:02:21.134 --> 00:02:22.453
a pattern to copy from.

00:02:23.094 --> 00:02:25.733
And because hydrogen bonds are weak, the strands

00:02:25.814 --> 00:02:28.933
can be pulled apart without breaking either backbone,

00:02:29.014 --> 00:02:32.533
which strong covalent bonds, where atoms share electrons,

00:02:32.654 --> 00:02:33.413
hold together.

00:02:34.634 --> 00:02:37.833
First, an enzyme, a protein that speeds up

00:02:37.874 --> 00:02:41.673
a reaction, called helicase unwinds the helix and

00:02:41.754 --> 00:02:44.313
breaks the hydrogen bonds, at a point called

00:02:44.354 --> 00:02:45.673
the replication fork.

00:02:46.314 --> 00:02:48.713
Each original strand is now a template.

00:02:49.514 --> 00:02:52.713
Second, loose nucleotides in the cell pair with

00:02:52.754 --> 00:02:55.913
the exposed bases, A with T and C

00:02:55.994 --> 00:02:56.553
with G.

00:02:57.274 --> 00:03:01.433
Third, an enzyme called DNA polymerase joins each

00:03:01.594 --> 00:03:04.873
new nucleotide to the last with covalent bonds,

00:03:04.954 --> 00:03:06.553
building the new backbone.

00:03:07.767 --> 00:03:10.806
Now we have two new DNA molecules, called

00:03:10.967 --> 00:03:14.406
daughter molecules, each with one original strand and

00:03:14.487 --> 00:03:15.526
one new strand.

00:03:16.087 --> 00:03:19.366
That is why this is called semi-conservative replication.

00:03:19.927 --> 00:03:23.606
Semi means half, and conservative means kept.

00:03:24.247 --> 00:03:26.566
It could have gone another way: one molecule

00:03:26.647 --> 00:03:29.126
could keep both original strands, and the other

00:03:29.207 --> 00:03:30.086
be all new.

00:03:30.567 --> 00:03:31.286
It does not.

00:03:31.767 --> 00:03:34.406
The original is shared out, one strand to

00:03:34.487 --> 00:03:34.886
each.

00:03:35.287 --> 00:03:37.046
Half old, half new.

00:03:38.267 --> 00:03:40.826
Let's copy a short piece of DNA, four

00:03:40.907 --> 00:03:42.426
pairs of bases long.

00:03:42.747 --> 00:03:44.746
This time the top strand is drawn the

00:03:44.827 --> 00:03:47.226
other way round, with its 3' end on

00:03:47.267 --> 00:03:50.666
the left, and reads A, T, C, G.

00:03:51.307 --> 00:03:55.226
Its partner underneath, 5' to 3', reads T,

00:03:55.387 --> 00:03:56.586
A, G, C.

00:03:57.307 --> 00:03:59.786
Step one: the strands separate.

00:04:00.267 --> 00:04:02.426
Both are templates now, so we colour them

00:04:02.507 --> 00:04:04.426
blue, because they are original.

00:04:05.634 --> 00:04:07.353
Start with the top template.

00:04:07.554 --> 00:04:09.873
Its first base is A, so the new

00:04:09.954 --> 00:04:11.313
strand needs a T.

00:04:11.874 --> 00:04:14.273
Then T needs an A, C needs a

00:04:14.354 --> 00:04:16.513
G, and G needs a C.

00:04:16.834 --> 00:04:20.273
The new strand, in green, reads T, A,

00:04:20.514 --> 00:04:22.913
G, C, and it runs opposite to its

00:04:22.994 --> 00:04:23.793
template.

00:04:24.114 --> 00:04:26.353
Now turn the pair round, so the template

00:04:26.434 --> 00:04:28.433
has its 5' end on the left.

00:04:28.914 --> 00:04:31.633
The letters swap order too: the template now

00:04:31.794 --> 00:04:34.753
reads G, C, T, A, and the new

00:04:34.834 --> 00:04:37.873
strand reads C, G, A, T from its

00:04:37.954 --> 00:04:38.993
3' end.

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:42.600
Now the bottom template, which has its 5'

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:45.680
end on the left and reads T, A,

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:46.680
G, C.

00:04:47.320 --> 00:04:50.120
T needs an A, A needs a T,

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:52.280
G needs a C, and C needs a

00:04:52.360 --> 00:04:52.760
G.

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:56.200
So the new top strand reads A, T,

00:04:56.280 --> 00:04:59.560
C, G, 3' end on the left, exactly

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:01.240
like the original top strand.

00:05:02.467 --> 00:05:04.346
Now put the molecules together.

00:05:04.627 --> 00:05:07.506
Each daughter has one blue original strand and

00:05:07.587 --> 00:05:11.026
one green new strand, and both read exactly

00:05:11.107 --> 00:05:12.706
like the molecule we began with.

00:05:13.267 --> 00:05:13.666
Why?

00:05:14.307 --> 00:05:16.706
Each new strand has the same bases as

00:05:16.747 --> 00:05:18.706
the original partner of its template.

00:05:19.187 --> 00:05:22.626
Each template rebuilds its missing partner, and that

00:05:22.707 --> 00:05:24.626
is how the information survives.

00:05:25.834 --> 00:05:28.793
Copying is accurate, but not perfect.

00:05:29.034 --> 00:05:31.753
Occasionally a wrong nucleotide is added.

00:05:32.074 --> 00:05:35.193
DNA polymerase checks each base and can back

00:05:35.274 --> 00:05:38.153
up to remove a mistake, and repair proteins

00:05:38.234 --> 00:05:41.193
check the new strand afterwards, so almost every

00:05:41.274 --> 00:05:43.033
mistake is fixed.

00:05:43.114 --> 00:05:45.833
One that survives can become a mutation, a

00:05:46.074 --> 00:05:47.273
change in the base order.

00:05:48.500 --> 00:05:51.700
Many mutations have no effect, some harm the

00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:54.100
cell, and a few are useful.

00:05:54.420 --> 00:05:57.140
When that DNA is copied again, the changed

00:05:57.220 --> 00:05:59.940
base gets its own partner, so one new

00:06:00.020 --> 00:06:02.180
copy has a changed base pair and the

00:06:02.260 --> 00:06:03.220
other is normal.

00:06:03.860 --> 00:06:06.580
That changed pair then passes to every cell

00:06:06.660 --> 00:06:07.900
that divides from it.

00:06:08.180 --> 00:06:10.820
In a bacterium that splits in two, only

00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:12.500
one of the two new cells gets the

00:06:12.580 --> 00:06:13.460
changed pair.

00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:16.900
In a person, a body cell's mutation passes

00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:19.140
only to the cells made from it, and

00:06:19.180 --> 00:06:21.140
reaches children only if it is in a

00:06:21.220 --> 00:06:22.740
cell that makes gametes.

00:06:23.967 --> 00:06:25.166
Two questions.

00:06:25.487 --> 00:06:28.926
One: this DNA template strand is written 5'

00:06:29.007 --> 00:06:33.526
to 3' and reads T, G, C, C,

00:06:33.647 --> 00:06:34.926
A, T.

00:06:35.047 --> 00:06:37.326
Write the new strand built beneath it, base

00:06:37.407 --> 00:06:39.846
for base, and label both ends.

00:06:40.287 --> 00:06:43.726
Two: both strands of one DNA molecule carry

00:06:43.807 --> 00:06:45.166
a heavy tag.

00:06:45.207 --> 00:06:47.806
It is copied once, then each new molecule

00:06:47.887 --> 00:06:49.646
is copied once more.

00:06:49.727 --> 00:06:52.926
New strands are always built from light nucleotides,

00:06:53.047 --> 00:06:55.806
and no strand is ever broken down.

00:06:55.887 --> 00:06:58.446
For each of the four final molecules, say

00:06:58.527 --> 00:07:00.606
how many strands are heavy and how many

00:07:00.687 --> 00:07:01.646
light.

00:07:01.807 --> 00:07:02.926
Pause here to answer.

00:07:13.334 --> 00:07:13.653
One.

00:07:13.734 --> 00:07:16.453
Pair each base with its partner.

00:07:16.694 --> 00:07:19.413
The template's 5' end is on the left,

00:07:19.494 --> 00:07:21.653
so the new strand's 3' end is on

00:07:21.734 --> 00:07:25.813
the left: A, C, G, G, T, A,

00:07:25.894 --> 00:07:27.733
from 3' to 5'.

00:07:28.374 --> 00:07:30.933
Read from its own 5' end, that is

00:07:31.334 --> 00:07:33.733
A, T, G, G, C, A.

00:07:34.374 --> 00:07:36.773
If you wrote A, T, G, G, C,

00:07:36.854 --> 00:07:39.373
A beneath the template from the left, the

00:07:39.414 --> 00:07:41.093
letters are the right ones, but they are

00:07:41.174 --> 00:07:43.973
in the wrong order beneath this template: that

00:07:44.054 --> 00:07:45.813
is how the new strand reads from its

00:07:45.894 --> 00:07:48.053
own 5' end, which is on the right.

00:07:48.694 --> 00:07:51.973
If you wrote U, that is RNA, and

00:07:52.134 --> 00:07:54.133
DNA uses T.

00:07:54.534 --> 00:07:56.933
If both strands have 5' on the left,

00:07:57.014 --> 00:07:58.773
they are not antiparallel.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:00.400
Two.

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:03.920
After the first copy there are two molecules,

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:06.800
each with one heavy original strand and one

00:08:06.880 --> 00:08:08.080
light new strand.

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:11.440
Copy those again, and each strand, heavy or

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:13.920
light, is a template for a new light

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:14.480
strand.

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:16.880
So you end with two molecules that have

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:19.760
one heavy and one light strand, and two

00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:21.200
with two light strands.

00:08:21.680 --> 00:08:24.160
If you said all four are partly heavy,

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:26.960
remember there are only two heavy strands, and

00:08:27.040 --> 00:08:28.400
they never multiply.

00:08:29.600 --> 00:08:32.400
Remember: half old, half new.

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:35.760
In every round, each new DNA molecule gets

00:08:35.840 --> 00:08:38.080
one old strand, the one it was copied

00:08:38.160 --> 00:08:40.400
from, and one new strand.

00:08:40.720 --> 00:08:42.880
The old strand might itself have been made

00:08:42.920 --> 00:08:44.000
a round earlier.

00:08:44.320 --> 00:08:47.600
The very first strands never multiply: after two

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:50.480
rounds, only two of the four molecules still

00:08:50.600 --> 00:08:51.560
hold one.

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:54.560
Next time: how a cell shares its copied

00:08:54.640 --> 00:08:56.880
DNA between two new cells.

00:08:58.100 --> 00:08:59.540
Here are the key notes.

00:08:59.780 --> 00:09:01.620
Pause the video if you need more time

00:09:01.700 --> 00:09:02.500
to copy them.
